-- Passage wg4-informative-writing-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('4710fdea-589f-5041-bfb7-55b8048a3137', 'wg4-informative-writing-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g4-informative-writing'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Bread Rises$q$, $q$(1) Have you ever wondered why bread is soft and full of tiny holes? (2) The answer is a living thing called yeast.

(3) Yeast is a tiny fungus. (4) It is so small that you need a microscope to see one by itself. (5) Bakers buy it dried, in small packets or jars. (6) When yeast is mixed with warm water, it wakes up and becomes active.

(7) A baker mixes yeast with flour, water and a little sugar to make dough. (8) The yeast eats the sugar. (9) As it eats, it gives off a gas. (10) The gas makes tiny bubbles. (11) The dough is stretchy, so it traps the bubbles. (12) The dough grows bigger and puffier.

(13) When the bread bakes in a hot oven, the bubbles leave little holes behind. (14) That is why each slice is light and soft.$q$, 'draft')
